  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nA cumulus field has developed this afternoon in two areas - \nacross central LA and south MS and across east central MS. Much \nlike yesterday, low level lapse rates are quite steep given the \nample surface heating and low level moisture. This will provide \npotential for another round of isolated to scattered storms this \nafternoon, with lower chances in our northern/western areas where \neven fair weather clouds are more sparse. DCAPE remains high \nacross the area, so wet microbursts resulting in damaging wind \ngusts will be possible in some storms, with greater odds further \nsouth where deep layer moisture is greater. Convection will \ndiminish in coverage after sunset.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: The remnant surface front will continue to\nstall and wash out over the next few days, with a Southeast US \nsurface ridge becoming a bigger player. The position of this high\nfavors the advection of a somewhat drier and...less hot...air \nmass being wedged around the Appalachians. What would this mean \nfor us? Temperatures that are still above average but not as high \nas what we have been seeing and increasingly marginal heat \nindices, with sites less likely to reach heat advisory criteria \neach day. As this modified airmass begins to move into the \neastern portion of the area tomorrow, we have clipped the heat \nadvisory to only cover our western half. And it's possible by \nWednesday an advisory will not be needed at all. Diurnal \nconvection is still possible each day where deep layer moisture\nwill be greater, favored over southern/western areas tomorrow \nthen across the south on Wednesday. \n\nThursday through next Monday: Another weak cold front will\napproach from the north at the end of the week but stall before\nreaching our area. Increasing deep layer moisture will pool south\nof this feature, resulting in higher rain chances over the back \nhalf of the forecast period. Though it will still be hot, greater \nprevalence of clouds and rain may continue to temper the heat \nenough to avoid critical heat stress thresholds being met this \nweekend into early next week. /DL/",
